1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
It's the fuzzy white lint that launched an industrial revolution

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,000
and sparked a civil war.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
When spun and woven, it clothes the human race.

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Its wondrous fibers and seeds make up everything from greenbacks

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:16,000
to blue jeans to lunch.

6
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
The only commodity to earn the title king.

7
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, Cotton on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,680
In late October, the cotton fields of California's San Joaquin Valley are bursting with white

9
00:00:41,681 --> 00:00:42,680
gold.

10
00:00:42,681 --> 00:00:43,880
It's harvest time.

11
00:00:43,881 --> 00:00:48,040
The ancient ritual of picking cotton is about to take place.

12
00:00:48,041 --> 00:00:54,760
But this cotton picker is made of steel.

13
00:00:54,761 --> 00:01:01,760
This is the John Deere 9996 Cotton Picker, which harvests six rows of cotton at once.

14
00:01:01,761 --> 00:01:08,760
Each machine does about 80 acres a day. We've got three of these machines.

15
00:01:08,761 --> 00:01:15,760
So we're doing anywhere from 220 to 240 acres in a day, which is a pretty good day.

16
00:01:15,761 --> 00:01:20,760
Despite its voracious appetite, this 8-ton machine is remarkably nimble.

17
00:01:20,761 --> 00:01:27,540
It uses 480 spindles attached to rotating drums to spool off the ripe cotton bowls.

18
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:30,500
The bowls are the fruit of the cotton plant.

19
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:38,500
A ripe bowl bursts open to reveal 500,000 fibers, known as lint, which are tightly attached to the seeds.

20
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:41,400
The rest of the plant is left intact.

21
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:49,620
And the spindle is turning and it has little barbs on it like a fish hook has.

22
00:01:49,621 --> 00:01:51,840
and it's also wet.

23
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:55,780
That's because a moisture column keeps each spindle lubricated.

24
00:01:56,840 --> 00:02:02,080
And so when it comes by here, it being wet will attract the lint,

25
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:05,600
then the little hooks will hook it and it'll wrap that around the spindle.

26
00:02:05,601 --> 00:02:07,040
Then it goes through a doffer.

27
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:11,860
The doffer helps clean the cotton off the spindle.

28
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:13,300
Then it goes through a vacuum.

29
00:02:13,980 --> 00:02:19,220
It's got a fan system on it that sucks the cotton through the tubes and into the basket.

30
00:02:19,620 --> 00:02:26,620
This ingenious design goes back to the very first one-row spindle picker designed in the

31
00:02:26,621 --> 00:02:27,620
1930s.

32
00:02:27,621 --> 00:02:35,620
And that one machine replaced somewhere around 50 hand-picking harvesters.

33
00:02:35,621 --> 00:02:42,620
Up until about the 1930s or beginning of the 1940s, most of the cotton was picked by hand.

34
00:02:42,621 --> 00:02:44,620
It was very difficult work.

35
00:02:44,621 --> 00:02:54,160
Modernization has really helped out tremendously.

36
00:02:54,161 --> 00:02:55,160
Bigger and better.

37
00:02:55,161 --> 00:02:59,900
Here at the Stone Land Company's cotton farm, the crop has immediately offloaded

38
00:02:59,901 --> 00:03:07,200
to another revolutionary machine, known as the Cotton Module Builder.

39
00:03:07,201 --> 00:03:12,060
Situated at the edge of the field, the Module Builder uses 3,000 pounds per square inch of

40
00:03:12,061 --> 00:03:19,260
hydraulic pressure to compress loose cotton bowls into dense blocks called modules.

41
00:03:19,261 --> 00:03:23,560
Each 20,000 pound module fits neatly into a custom-built truck that can go from the

42
00:03:23,561 --> 00:03:25,560
field right to the cotton gin.

43
00:03:25,561 --> 00:03:33,420
The cotton module builder was significant because it cut down the number of trips that

44
00:03:33,421 --> 00:03:38,300
the farmers would make hauling their cotton in small trailers or wagons.

45
00:03:38,301 --> 00:03:44,680
It could haul at least twice the volume and weight of cotton from the field to the gin,

46
00:03:44,681 --> 00:03:49,200
probably three to four times faster.

47
00:03:49,201 --> 00:03:53,503
With its remarkable growing conditions, California has emerged as a powerhouse for U.S.

48
00:03:53,504 --> 00:03:54,204
cotton

49
00:03:54,205 --> 00:03:58,120
cultivation, second only to Texas.

50
00:03:58,121 --> 00:04:03,180
San Joaquin Valley farmers specialize in high-quality varieties of upland and pima cotton.

51
00:04:03,181 --> 00:04:06,800
It will end up in dress shirts and high-thread couch sheets.

52
00:04:06,801 --> 00:04:11,800
It is a little bit longer and a little bit stronger than most upland varieties

53
00:04:11,801 --> 00:04:15,700
and the way we accomplish that is we have a much longer growing season. We

54
00:04:15,701 --> 00:04:20,240
plant our cotton in March and we harvest in October and plus all the cotton in

55
00:04:20,241 --> 00:04:24,800
California and the San Joaquin Valley is irrigated so we control our inputs much

56
00:04:24,801 --> 00:04:29,900
more so than in a lot of places in the United States. As soon as they've been

57
00:04:29,901 --> 00:04:33,640
harvested these fields will quickly be converted to other crops for the sake of

58
00:04:33,641 --> 00:04:40,040
the soil. Cotton rapidly depletes the soil of key nutrients, especially boron.

59
00:04:40,041 --> 00:04:47,460
Crop rotation is very important and we probably average two years of cotton and then a year

60
00:04:47,461 --> 00:04:56,200
or two of another crop like garlic or onions or wheat, cantaloupe or processing tomatoes.

61
00:04:56,201 --> 00:05:02,520
From soil care to irrigation to pest control, cotton is a high maintenance crop and the

62
00:05:02,521 --> 00:05:07,520
The harvest is only the beginning of cotton's long journey.

63
00:05:07,521 --> 00:05:10,520
This is a cotton gin.

64
00:05:10,521 --> 00:05:15,520
To be specific, the KDW cotton gin in Bakersfield, California.

65
00:05:15,521 --> 00:05:18,520
At one end of the gin, freshly picked cotton,

66
00:05:18,521 --> 00:05:21,520
still stuck to its seeds and peppered with plant debris,

67
00:05:21,521 --> 00:05:23,520
known as trash.

68
00:05:23,521 --> 00:05:26,520
At the other end, bales of pure cotton lint,

69
00:05:26,521 --> 00:05:29,520
ready for the textile mill.

70
00:05:29,521 --> 00:05:34,520
The transformation begins as the giant cotton modules are fed into a disperser,

71
00:05:34,521 --> 00:05:38,520
which breaks the cotton up and conveys it to an airstream.

72
00:05:38,521 --> 00:05:41,520
The cotton is conveyed by air throughout the entire facility.

73
00:05:41,521 --> 00:05:44,520
We do that to keep it from basically nodding up.

74
00:05:44,521 --> 00:05:49,520
We have about 30 million BTUs of heat we use in this particular facility

75
00:05:49,521 --> 00:05:55,520
to start heating the cotton and warming it up into the low to mid-hundred degree range,

76
00:05:55,521 --> 00:05:58,520
which is enough to allow that fiber to relax.

77
00:05:58,521 --> 00:06:04,520
When those fibers start relaxing, it allows us to use gravity cleaners,

78
00:06:04,521 --> 00:06:12,520
whereas we're basically shaking the cotton to let the leaf and materials fall out of it.

79
00:06:12,521 --> 00:06:20,520
The cotton is then fed into the gin stand, which performs a simple task that vexed cotton farmers for centuries,

80
00:06:20,521 --> 00:06:25,520
separating the cotton lint from the seed quickly and continuously.

81
00:06:25,521 --> 00:06:31,700
At the heart of the cotton gin are rotating saw blades, which grab the lint and pull it

82
00:06:31,701 --> 00:06:33,700
through a narrow opening.

83
00:06:33,701 --> 00:06:40,280
A gin could have anywhere from 90 of these up to 171 of these in each particular gin

84
00:06:40,281 --> 00:06:45,400
stand of which this location has five gin stands.

85
00:06:45,401 --> 00:06:49,420
These turn at a rate of speed of about 12 to 1400 RPM.

86
00:06:49,421 --> 00:06:53,300
And basically you'll just see just a small amount of the saw actually sticks through

87
00:06:53,301 --> 00:06:56,880
just enough to be able to grab that fiber and then pull it through.

88
00:06:57,400 --> 00:07:00,680
The seeds, too large to pass through the ribs, fall below.

89
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:06,640
The cotton fibers are combed, cleaned again, and then smoothed into a layer called a bat.

90
00:07:07,940 --> 00:07:12,660
A hydraulic press squeezes the cotton lint into 485-pound pails,

91
00:07:13,360 --> 00:07:15,940
still the standard unit of cotton after hundreds of years.

92
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:22,820
Meanwhile, the gin has separated the plant trash and cottonseed into two small mountains.

93
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:25,960
Every scrap of these products will be used.

94
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:32,160
This fluffy white commodity is quite simply one of the most useful substances on Earth.

95
00:07:33,960 --> 00:07:37,720
You know, everybody uses it, everybody wears it, nobody even thinks about it.

96
00:07:38,060 --> 00:07:45,480
But that is where it accumulates power, because there are so many ways in which cotton becomes a part of our life,

97
00:07:45,481 --> 00:07:50,780
whether it's something like a t-shirt, jeans, and so forth, that when you put all of that together,

98
00:07:50,781 --> 00:07:54,460
the aggregated power of cotton comes from its ubiquitous use.

99
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:58,220
Cotton is something that you're born in, almost.

100
00:07:58,520 --> 00:07:59,220
Your first diapers.

101
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:05,220
I don't think the new mothers would be interested in putting it in any other fabric but cotton.

102
00:08:05,580 --> 00:08:10,200
But in every stage, from the morning till night, including the sheets you sleep on, are cotton.

103
00:08:11,900 --> 00:08:15,660
Estimates suggest as many as 20 million cotton producers around the world.

104
00:08:15,661 --> 00:08:21,220
And then we have estimated perhaps 5,000 to 10,000 textile mills globally as well.

105
00:08:21,760 --> 00:08:24,540
And not to mention the retail community that's ultimately selling it.

106
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:30,320
Cotton has grown in about 100 countries, and it's processed in about 120 countries around the world.

107
00:08:30,480 --> 00:08:34,800
There's about 130 billion pounds of fiber used every year.

108
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:40,480
About 95% of every cotton bale will end up as clothing or home textiles.

109
00:08:40,840 --> 00:08:44,500
One of the things that makes cotton unique is it's actually a hollow fiber.

110
00:08:44,501 --> 00:08:50,300
So people know that cotton absorbs moisture, and one of the reasons is it has room in the center.

111
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:58,200
It does also have an insulating value from the standpoint of creating an airspace within the fiber itself,

112
00:08:58,340 --> 00:09:01,320
and so it's warm in the winter and cool in the summer.

113
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:03,960
That's just one of cotton's many talents.

114
00:09:04,440 --> 00:09:10,560
One of the things that makes cotton so useful is that anatomically the cotton fibers twist and spiral.

115
00:09:10,561 --> 00:09:12,440
That means that they cling together.

116
00:09:12,441 --> 00:09:21,020
And because they cling together, they can be formed into very long chains, which ultimately are reduced from fluffy to thread size.

117
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:27,620
These strong cellulose fibers also go into everything from couch stuffings to candle wigs.

118
00:09:28,420 --> 00:09:34,560
And through the wonders of modern chemistry, plastics, explosives, even photographic film.

119
00:09:35,620 --> 00:09:40,520
And if you ate some potato chips today, chances are they contained oil derived from the cottonseed.

120
00:09:42,440 --> 00:09:47,160
But it's cotton's performance as cloth that first got the attention of the human race.

121
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:51,360
It began in the northwest part of India, the Indus Valley.

122
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:56,540
Tiny pieces of fiber have been found there that indicate that as many as 5,500 years ago,

123
00:09:57,300 --> 00:10:00,000
cotton was domesticated and actually made into cloth in that area.

124
00:10:01,740 --> 00:10:04,880
A second species of cotton was bred in South America.

125
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:08,800
These plants often produced naturally pigmented bowls.

126
00:10:08,801 --> 00:10:12,640
And while the Inca are known for their textile mastery,

127
00:10:13,400 --> 00:10:18,420
the use of cotton textiles in the Andes actually predates the Inca by 2,000 years.

128
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:24,420
A third species was bred in Central America and a fourth in East Africa.

129
00:10:25,340 --> 00:10:28,500
These four are the progenitors of the modern cotton plant.

130
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:31,120
It's a perennial plant.

131
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:34,900
If the cold weather didn't come along, cotton would grow into a tree.

132
00:10:34,901 --> 00:10:45,700
The world's first cotton industry took root in India, and by the time Alexander the Great arrived in India in 320 BC, cotton had become the predominant fabric.

133
00:10:47,540 --> 00:10:51,598
Alexander and his soldiers used Indian cotton to pad their saddles.

134
00:10:51,599 --> 00:10:53,900
They called the soft fabric tree wool.

135
00:10:55,220 --> 00:10:59,300
Europeans' confusion over cotton's origins continued well into the modern era.

136
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:06,840
Much of the blame for this confusion can be attributed to the imaginative 15th century travel writer, John Mandeville,

137
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:11,460
who wrote in great detail about the dewy sheep that grew on trees.

138
00:11:12,120 --> 00:11:15,960
And as these dewy little sheep bent down to eat the grass,

139
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:21,800
they collapsed and fell over, leaving behind the dew, the dewy fluff on the plant.

140
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:27,340
So the vegetable lambs of scythia, as he called them,

141
00:11:28,180 --> 00:11:29,940
he considered to be the source of cotton.

142
00:11:30,660 --> 00:11:32,000
And everybody bought this story.

143
00:11:32,860 --> 00:11:34,800
And this story kept resurfacing throughout history,

144
00:11:35,140 --> 00:11:37,180
even 200 to 300 years later.

145
00:11:38,360 --> 00:11:41,520
Arabian traders called the cloth from India kutun,

146
00:11:42,060 --> 00:11:43,240
meaning fine fabric.

147
00:11:43,880 --> 00:11:45,980
The Anglicized name became cotton.

148
00:11:46,980 --> 00:11:49,540
Cotton itself was nearly nonexistent in Europe,

149
00:11:49,541 --> 00:11:52,760
which relied on linen and especially wool for its clothing.

150
00:11:54,100 --> 00:11:59,260
The tide began to turn with Vasco da Gama's 16th century voyages to Calcutta.

151
00:12:00,320 --> 00:12:05,000
Vivid Indian textiles, which miraculously retained their deep colors after repeated washing,

152
00:12:05,580 --> 00:12:07,900
began to find their way to Europe's upper classes.

153
00:12:09,140 --> 00:12:13,500
Suddenly, the wealthy people of Europe, in England in particular, and France,

154
00:12:13,880 --> 00:12:18,600
all the wealthy women wanted to have as much of this colorful cotton as possible around.

155
00:12:18,601 --> 00:12:21,400
And everything started to change from that moment on.

156
00:12:21,940 --> 00:12:27,080
By 1700, the cotton craze had become such a threat to wool growers in England and France

157
00:12:27,081 --> 00:12:32,020
that wearing cotton was made a crime, punishable in some cases by death.

158
00:12:33,660 --> 00:12:36,720
Still, the desire for cotton filtered into the rest of the population.

159
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:40,360
Soon demand far outpaced supply.

160
00:12:41,420 --> 00:12:45,900
England's King George II began to eye the cotton fields of India with envy.

161
00:12:45,901 --> 00:12:50,900
Many people wonder why England took over India in the middle of the 18th century.

162
00:12:50,901 --> 00:12:52,900
And the answer is pretty simple.

163
00:12:52,901 --> 00:12:55,900
India had a huge amount of resources that England wanted,

164
00:12:55,901 --> 00:12:58,900
and England was kind of tired of having to pay for them.

165
00:12:58,901 --> 00:13:00,900
So what do you do when you don't want to pay for something?

166
00:13:00,901 --> 00:13:02,900
You take over the country.

167
00:13:02,901 --> 00:13:04,900
That was very true of cotton.

168
00:13:04,901 --> 00:13:09,900
It wasn't the only reason by any means that England took over India, overran it really.

169
00:13:09,901 --> 00:13:13,900
Created a false war and then used that as a pretense to take over the country.

170
00:13:13,901 --> 00:13:16,400
country, but it was one of the main reasons.

171
00:13:16,401 --> 00:13:22,600
From the 1750s on, England forbade Indian cotton growers from spinning or weaving their

172
00:13:22,601 --> 00:13:29,473
own product, forcing them to buy cloth from the new English textile mills.

173
00:13:29,474 --> 00:13:30,680
Indian cotton

174
00:13:30,681 --> 00:13:35,367
would remain in British hands until the mid-20th century, when Mohandas K.

175
00:13:35,368 --> 00:13:36,380
Gandhi urged his

176
00:13:36,381 --> 00:13:44,880
countrymen to take the symbolic and very real action of once again spinning their own cotton.

177
00:13:44,881 --> 00:13:48,747
But in the late 18th century, that day was a long way off.

178
00:13:48,748 --> 00:13:50,480
England had secured itself

179
00:13:50,481 --> 00:13:52,512
a bounty of Indian cotton.

180
00:13:52,513 --> 00:13:58,140
Soon it would possess the means to turn it into cloth quickly. England's

181
00:13:58,141 --> 00:14:04,460
textile mills and America's cotton gin would jumpstart an industrial revolution and ignite

182
00:14:04,461 --> 00:14:06,060
a looming civil war.

183
00:14:09,020 --> 00:14:10,700
Indy's cotton textile tradition

184
00:14:10,701 --> 00:14:12,260
has given the English language

185
00:14:12,261 --> 00:14:16,740
the words pajama, dungaree, seersucker, calico,

186
00:14:17,420 --> 00:14:19,000
madras, and khaki.

187
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:23,020
Cotton will return on Modern Marvels

188
00:14:23,021 --> 00:14:24,720
here on the History Channel.

189
00:14:30,160 --> 00:14:33,040
You're looking at a machine that changed history.

190
00:14:34,460 --> 00:14:39,460
Today's high-speed, high-tech cotton gin belies its humble origins.

191
00:14:39,461 --> 00:14:46,460
It all began with a breakthrough made in 1793 by an unemployed Yankee tinkerer named Eli Whitney.

192
00:14:46,461 --> 00:14:50,460
In the very largest gin to the very smallest gin, the concept's the same.

193
00:14:50,461 --> 00:14:55,460
You have ribs, which are these here, saw blades, and a slot.

194
00:14:55,461 --> 00:15:00,460
And the cotton is grabbed by the saw blade and pulled through the slot.

195
00:15:00,461 --> 00:15:05,215
the slot, but the seed is too big to go through that slot.

196
00:15:05,216 --> 00:15:07,920
These are the brushes turning and

197
00:15:07,921 --> 00:15:12,460
they get the lint off of the back of the saw blades, collect it, they produce a lot of

198
00:15:12,461 --> 00:15:17,540
wind, it blows the cotton out of the back of the gin.

199
00:15:17,541 --> 00:15:21,580
Before Eli Whitney's gin, cotton had been an unprofitable specialty crop in the United

200
00:15:21,581 --> 00:15:22,987
States. The problem?

201
00:15:22,988 --> 00:15:27,840
It took up to 18 months for a slave to remove the seeds from one bale

202
00:15:27,841 --> 00:15:28,541
of cotton.

203
00:15:28,542 --> 00:15:33,040
And so no matter how much cotton could be grown, there was no way in the whole

204
00:15:33,041 --> 00:15:38,900
supply chain that it could be converted as quickly as necessary through all the various

205
00:15:38,901 --> 00:15:40,828
stages to make it into a fabric.

206
00:15:40,829 --> 00:15:43,960
Whitney's invention sped up the ginning process by a

207
00:15:43,961 --> 00:15:44,746
factor of 50.

208
00:15:44,747 --> 00:15:49,340
Ultimately that winds up being a thousand to two thousand times more because

209
00:15:49,341 --> 00:15:55,480
of the use of steam engines and so forth. In 1793, the year of Whitney's invention,

210
00:15:56,280 --> 00:15:58,700
The South produced 10,000 bales of cotton.

211
00:15:59,460 --> 00:16:02,980
By 1835, it was producing 1 million bales a year.

212
00:16:03,860 --> 00:16:05,440
Cotton had become king.

213
00:16:06,500 --> 00:16:13,180
By mid-century, the once insignificant crop would account for nearly two-thirds of the total value of American exports.

214
00:16:14,940 --> 00:16:19,640
Cotton plantations bloomed across the South, especially along the Mississippi Delta.

215
00:16:19,641 --> 00:16:24,020
It was flat, had a lot of average waterfall and rain,

216
00:16:24,540 --> 00:16:28,440
and rich soils that would grow just about anything they wanted to.

217
00:16:28,720 --> 00:16:32,500
But the bounty made possible by the cotton gin came at a terrible price.

218
00:16:33,320 --> 00:16:35,180
In making cotton economically viable,

219
00:16:35,660 --> 00:16:39,340
it also created a huge demand for slave labor to pick the cotton.

220
00:16:40,340 --> 00:16:44,640
Slavery was on the way out for a short time in the late 1700s,

221
00:16:45,280 --> 00:16:46,740
but along came Whitney's Gin,

222
00:16:46,741 --> 00:16:49,860
and then all of a sudden slavery was back in vogue.

223
00:16:50,080 --> 00:16:53,800
As cotton began to deplete the soil of the southeastern states,

224
00:16:54,300 --> 00:16:57,100
the plantation owners, rather than using crop rotation,

225
00:16:57,740 --> 00:17:00,780
moved further west, planted more cotton,

226
00:17:01,380 --> 00:17:02,900
and brought in more slaves.

227
00:17:04,440 --> 00:17:07,220
Whitney's gin not only made cotton a viable crop,

228
00:17:07,580 --> 00:17:09,860
it enabled innovations taking place in England

229
00:17:09,861 --> 00:17:11,980
to make textiles a booming industry.

230
00:17:11,981 --> 00:17:20,860
The first major innovation was James Hargrave's Spinning Jenny, which mechanized the ancient

231
00:17:20,861 --> 00:17:24,480
art of spinning and twisting fluffy cotton fibers into strong yarn.

232
00:17:25,480 --> 00:17:30,400
But it was the tireless entrepreneur Richard Arkwright who perfected Hargrave's spinning

233
00:17:30,401 --> 00:17:34,460
machines and incorporated them into the world's first modern factories.

234
00:17:35,460 --> 00:17:41,360
When Richard Arkwright, the father of the Industrial Revolution, came along in about 1770, he took

235
00:17:41,361 --> 00:17:45,580
the best qualities of Hargrave's spinning jenny,

236
00:17:45,581 --> 00:17:47,980
combined them with a few other innovations

237
00:17:47,981 --> 00:17:49,820
that were going nowhere, really,

238
00:17:49,821 --> 00:17:54,500
and created the first mechanical spinning apparatus

239
00:17:54,501 --> 00:17:55,840
called the waterframe.

240
00:17:55,841 --> 00:17:57,000
It was called the waterframe

241
00:17:57,001 --> 00:18:00,780
because the rivers of northern England provided the power.

242
00:18:00,781 --> 00:18:04,680
These rivers drove a crucial early 19th century innovation,

243
00:18:04,681 --> 00:18:08,060
the power loom, which wove cotton yarn into fabric

244
00:18:08,061 --> 00:18:10,060
at a pace unthinkable by a hand weaver.

245
00:18:12,060 --> 00:18:15,720
When fabric is woven, vertical yarns, called the warp,

246
00:18:16,040 --> 00:18:18,980
are intersected by horizontal yarns, called the weft.

247
00:18:20,800 --> 00:18:22,660
Harnesses, also called heddles,

248
00:18:23,340 --> 00:18:24,840
separate the lines of warp yarn

249
00:18:24,841 --> 00:18:27,200
to create a space for the weft to pass through.

250
00:18:27,900 --> 00:18:30,700
This is two heddles, two harnesses,

251
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:32,840
which makes a plain weave.

252
00:18:33,300 --> 00:18:35,280
When one goes up, the other goes down,

253
00:18:35,600 --> 00:18:37,140
and it forms what we call a shed,

254
00:18:37,141 --> 00:18:38,440
and it's like a little lean-to.

255
00:18:38,740 --> 00:18:41,520
The shed is the area the shuttle will fly through.

256
00:18:42,100 --> 00:18:43,600
When the shuttle flies through it,

257
00:18:43,760 --> 00:18:45,900
from the bobbin it leaves what we call a pick.

258
00:18:46,420 --> 00:18:47,360
That's one pick.

259
00:18:48,400 --> 00:18:52,180
It's all powered by a clutch system with a handle here.

260
00:18:52,480 --> 00:18:54,680
When you pull the handle, it engages the clutch,

261
00:18:55,080 --> 00:18:57,060
driven by the overhead line shaft and the pulley.

262
00:18:57,480 --> 00:19:00,760
We'll turn the crankshaft, which will spin the driveshaft,

263
00:19:00,860 --> 00:19:02,740
which has cams on it that'll throw the shuttle

264
00:19:02,741 --> 00:19:03,880
from one side to the other.

265
00:19:04,760 --> 00:19:08,240
Cotton textiles were the vanguard of the Industrial Revolution.

266
00:19:10,640 --> 00:19:13,700
Cotton factories sprouted alongside England's rivers

267
00:19:13,701 --> 00:19:16,600
as quickly as cotton plantations along the Mississippi.

268
00:19:18,680 --> 00:19:22,240
This revolution turned the sleepy town of Manchester, England,

269
00:19:22,680 --> 00:19:24,520
into an industrial giant overnight.

270
00:19:25,160 --> 00:19:28,340
And by the way, it becomes one of the great foul cities on Earth

271
00:19:28,341 --> 00:19:30,180
because there were no regulations.

272
00:19:30,181 --> 00:19:32,620
It also becomes socially reprehensible

273
00:19:32,621 --> 00:19:36,420
because kids at the age of six or seven were forced to work in factories.

274
00:19:37,040 --> 00:19:40,480
Over 200,000 children in England, almost all of them orphans,

275
00:19:40,720 --> 00:19:44,980
were pressed into work in the cotton factories of Manchester.

276
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,260
They weren't just sweatshops, they were prisons for kids.

277
00:19:49,020 --> 00:19:52,300
After touring the cotton mills of Manchester in 1812,

278
00:19:52,301 --> 00:19:56,040
a Massachusetts entrepreneur named Francis Cabot Lowell

279
00:19:56,041 --> 00:20:00,660
vowed to bring England's technology home, but without the human suffering.

280
00:20:00,661 --> 00:20:05,660
Lowell helped found the first great industrial towns of America.

281
00:20:05,661 --> 00:20:10,660
Francis Cabot Lowell and the Boston Associates, the Boston aristocrats, built the first factory

282
00:20:10,661 --> 00:20:12,154
in Waltham, Massachusetts.

283
00:20:12,155 --> 00:20:15,660
Lowell then dies in his early 40s, but his brother-in-law and

284
00:20:15,661 --> 00:20:21,660
the rest of the associates built the first real big factory in the United States in Lowell,

285
00:20:21,661 --> 00:20:25,660
Massachusetts, because it's next to the Pawtucket Falls.

286
00:20:25,661 --> 00:20:28,660
Unlike the enslaved child workers of Manchester,

287
00:20:28,661 --> 00:20:33,660
the Yankee farm girls who toiled in Lowell were well cared for by the Boston Associates.

288
00:20:33,661 --> 00:20:36,660
Their best workers were young women,

289
00:20:36,661 --> 00:20:40,660
and in order to get these young women over here by the hundreds and thousands,

290
00:20:40,661 --> 00:20:44,660
they had to have an environment that their parents would allow them to work in.

291
00:20:46,660 --> 00:20:49,660
The power loom continued to evolve.

292
00:20:49,661 --> 00:20:53,660
Pre-programmed pattern chains, like the pegs in a music box,

293
00:20:53,661 --> 00:20:55,660
churned out fancier weeds.

294
00:20:55,661 --> 00:20:59,660
Automatic bobbin-changing devices allowed for ever-increasing speed.

295
00:20:59,661 --> 00:21:02,660
We judge loom speed five-picks per minute.

296
00:21:02,661 --> 00:21:05,660
These are running about 150-picks per minute.

297
00:21:05,661 --> 00:21:09,660
As loom speeds increased, so did the demand for cotton.

298
00:21:09,661 --> 00:21:14,660
But the endless flow of slave-picked cotton into New England's textile mills

299
00:21:14,661 --> 00:21:17,660
was becoming too hard to ignore.

300
00:21:17,661 --> 00:21:21,660
Northern abolitionists, including many in Lowell,

301
00:21:21,661 --> 00:21:27,300
in Lowell cried out against the unholy alliance between southern cotton planters, the lords

302
00:21:27,301 --> 00:21:32,560
of the lash, and the northern textile barons, the lords of the loom.

303
00:21:32,561 --> 00:21:38,980
There was so much money on the table that it made no sense for the lords of the loom,

304
00:21:39,540 --> 00:21:43,740
the industrialists of New England, to try to stop slavery in the south, at least where

305
00:21:43,741 --> 00:21:44,441
it existed.

306
00:21:44,880 --> 00:21:48,100
And they went through contortions to try to justify that because they were often very

307
00:21:48,101 --> 00:21:52,433
religious people who had a moral apprehension about slavery.

308
00:21:52,434 --> 00:21:54,960
But at the same time, economically,

309
00:21:55,120 --> 00:21:56,413
it made great sense.

310
00:21:56,414 --> 00:22:00,680
It was the cooperation of those two forces that kept the Civil War

311
00:22:00,681 --> 00:22:05,520
from happening a lot earlier than it did, at least as I reread history.

312
00:22:07,360 --> 00:22:10,860
As soon as anti-slavery forces tipped the delicate balance,

313
00:22:11,580 --> 00:22:13,420
the nation's bloodiest war began.

314
00:22:18,100 --> 00:22:25,860
Since cotton was the southern economy, it played a major role in deciding the war's

315
00:22:25,861 --> 00:22:26,860
outcome.

316
00:22:26,861 --> 00:22:30,520
One of the mistakes that the south made, that turned out to be the most devastating

317
00:22:30,521 --> 00:22:35,220
mistake in many ways, was that it was absolutely sure, because so much of the cotton, two thirds

318
00:22:35,221 --> 00:22:39,220
of the cotton being grown in the south were going to feed the English mills, there was

319
00:22:39,221 --> 00:22:44,300
no way, according to the plantation owners, that England could do anything except support

320
00:22:44,301 --> 00:22:46,380
the south in the civil war.

321
00:22:46,381 --> 00:22:48,380
And that makes perfect sense economically.

322
00:22:48,381 --> 00:22:55,060
But ultimately, the anti-slavery factions of England prevailed over the manufacturing

323
00:22:55,061 --> 00:22:56,740
interests.

324
00:22:56,741 --> 00:23:04,560
And England refused to support the South in the Civil War by continuing to buy its cotton.

325
00:23:04,561 --> 00:23:08,420
And it turned out that it went back to India and bought all the cotton that it needed,

326
00:23:08,421 --> 00:23:11,420
or got all the cotton it needed from India instead.

327
00:23:11,421 --> 00:23:16,200
After the destruction of the Civil War, it would take many decades, and some help from

328
00:23:16,201 --> 00:23:19,960
a pair of jeans and a t-shirt for cotton to become king again.

329
00:23:20,540 --> 00:23:24,160
But first, it would have to defeat its greatest enemy of all.

330
00:23:25,740 --> 00:23:29,780
The shocking conditions in England's 19th century textile mills

331
00:23:29,781 --> 00:23:32,840
influenced the reformist writings of Charles Dickens,

332
00:23:33,400 --> 00:23:37,160
as well as the Communist Manifesto of Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels.

333
00:23:37,880 --> 00:23:40,000
Engels' family owned a textile mill in England.

334
00:23:41,300 --> 00:23:43,820
Cotton will return on Modern Marvels.

335
00:23:46,200 --> 00:23:50,120
The Civil War cost King Cotton his crown,

336
00:23:50,600 --> 00:23:53,480
but an insect brought him to his knees.

337
00:23:54,960 --> 00:23:58,200
The tiny flying insect known as the bull weevil

338
00:23:58,201 --> 00:24:01,580
crossed into the U.S. from Mexico in the 1890s.

339
00:24:02,380 --> 00:24:07,180
It would destroy some $14 billion worth of cotton in the 20th century.

340
00:24:08,040 --> 00:24:10,860
Cotton is the perfect home for a variety of pests.

341
00:24:10,861 --> 00:24:16,080
A cotton bull opens up to about 500,000 fibers

342
00:24:16,081 --> 00:24:17,120
that grow inside it.

343
00:24:17,400 --> 00:24:24,540
The inside of that pod is like a perfect Ritz-Carlton hotel for insects.

344
00:24:24,780 --> 00:24:25,980
It's warm and cuddly.

345
00:24:26,360 --> 00:24:27,660
They can lay their eggs there,

346
00:24:27,800 --> 00:24:30,140
and then when they're ready to hatch, they can crawl out.

347
00:24:30,620 --> 00:24:35,080
The Wiley boll weevil managed to beat every pesticide farmers threw at it.

348
00:24:35,600 --> 00:24:38,180
A long time ago, the only way to combat the boll weevil

349
00:24:38,181 --> 00:24:39,860
was to use arsenic of lead,

350
00:24:40,280 --> 00:24:43,080
and they colored it green, so they called it Paris Green.

351
00:24:43,081 --> 00:24:47,080
They would fill both of these hoppers with the Paris green.

352
00:24:47,081 --> 00:24:50,080
And then to get on the back of a mule and they would ride it,

353
00:24:50,081 --> 00:24:53,080
or sometimes walk and shake it, is they went through the field.

354
00:24:53,081 --> 00:24:57,080
And it would get the dust all over the plants early in the morning when there was dew.

355
00:24:57,081 --> 00:25:02,080
And it would combat the weevil and kill it, but it didn't work very well.

356
00:25:02,081 --> 00:25:05,080
It wouldn't kill all the weevils, but it's all that they had.

357
00:25:05,081 --> 00:25:09,080
From the 1920s on, farmers using more sophisticated insecticides

358
00:25:09,081 --> 00:25:13,722
insecticides would gain the upper hand, only to see the weevil return.

359
00:25:13,723 --> 00:25:15,380
The big break came in the

360
00:25:15,381 --> 00:25:20,768
1970s when scientists discovered how to isolate the weevil's pheromones.

361
00:25:20,769 --> 00:25:22,340
Farmers set pheromone

362
00:25:22,341 --> 00:25:26,596
traps to lure the weevil population into certain areas of the cotton fields.

363
00:25:26,597 --> 00:25:27,660
With the weevils in

364
00:25:27,661 --> 00:25:30,912
one spot, spraying became much more effective.

365
00:25:30,913 --> 00:25:33,740
The legendary bull weevil was contained,

366
00:25:34,160 --> 00:25:36,840
But not until the late 1980s.

367
00:25:37,180 --> 00:25:38,220
It took them a century.

368
00:25:38,420 --> 00:25:41,160
During that time, we sent a person to the moon.

369
00:25:41,420 --> 00:25:43,140
We developed nuclear energy.

370
00:25:43,400 --> 00:25:45,080
We did all sorts of things, the automobile.

371
00:25:45,640 --> 00:25:47,520
During that entire span of the 20th century,

372
00:25:47,600 --> 00:25:50,920
nobody could figure out how to wipe out this tiny little bug.

373
00:25:52,700 --> 00:25:55,020
The weevil-ravaged economy of the South

374
00:25:55,021 --> 00:25:57,620
received a shot in the arm in the early 20th century

375
00:25:57,621 --> 00:25:59,880
when the textile mills of New England

376
00:25:59,881 --> 00:26:03,700
began to relocate to Georgia, Alabama, and the Carolinas.

377
00:26:03,740 --> 00:26:11,100
One of these mills is Cone Denim's White Oak Plant in Greensboro, North Carolina, which

378
00:26:11,101 --> 00:26:14,020
has been turning raw cotton into denim since 1905.

379
00:26:16,300 --> 00:26:20,600
During that time, the White Oak Plant has made enough denim for six billion pairs of

380
00:26:20,601 --> 00:26:21,301
jeans.

381
00:26:23,560 --> 00:26:29,060
At a modern textile mill, the process of converting cotton to cloth begins as a machine called

382
00:26:29,061 --> 00:26:33,500
a top feeder sweeps the cotton from bales and sucks it into a cleaning machine.

383
00:26:33,740 --> 00:26:38,380
which opens up the fibers and removes any remaining trash.

384
00:26:39,720 --> 00:26:43,840
To make cotton into yarn, you first have to align the fibers in one direction

385
00:26:43,841 --> 00:26:46,180
through a process called carting.

386
00:26:47,220 --> 00:26:50,440
We're starting out with unoriented fiber,

387
00:26:50,960 --> 00:26:57,160
and then this machine, through these working surfaces that you see on top here,

388
00:26:59,320 --> 00:27:03,680
these working surfaces are coming in contact with the fiber on the cylinder,

389
00:27:03,740 --> 00:27:09,900
and basically doing the same thing that we used to do hundreds of years ago

390
00:27:09,901 --> 00:27:12,360
with these hand tools called hand cards.

391
00:27:12,361 --> 00:27:18,920
It's aligning, straightening, and paralleling that fiber into what we call a sliver.

392
00:27:20,560 --> 00:27:24,520
And this is the beginning, then, of making a yarn.

393
00:27:25,680 --> 00:27:30,360
The sliver is spooled into cans, drawn out and recombined,

394
00:27:30,361 --> 00:27:33,700
then pulled into thin strands called roving,

395
00:27:34,460 --> 00:27:36,180
which are wound onto bobbins.

396
00:27:37,900 --> 00:27:40,480
It's now time for a very high-tech version

397
00:27:40,481 --> 00:27:42,160
of the ancient art of spinning yarn.

398
00:27:43,640 --> 00:27:46,060
All yarns have one thing in common,

399
00:27:46,240 --> 00:27:47,540
no matter what spinning system,

400
00:27:47,640 --> 00:27:49,300
and that is that they all have twists.

401
00:27:50,060 --> 00:27:54,600
We can do a little demo here with our hand here.

402
00:27:54,660 --> 00:27:55,920
You see how weak that is.

403
00:27:55,921 --> 00:27:57,680
If we think about that being a yarn,

404
00:27:57,681 --> 00:28:03,300
then all we have to do is if we apply just a little bit of twist to that fiber,

405
00:28:04,100 --> 00:28:06,480
and if you grip it up here and let's pull on that,

406
00:28:07,080 --> 00:28:09,660
you have a very strong material, you see.

407
00:28:10,780 --> 00:28:14,060
At the White Oak plant, about 12,000 spindles

408
00:28:14,061 --> 00:28:17,500
twist the roving into yarn on machines called ring spinners.

409
00:28:18,120 --> 00:28:21,800
At this point in the process, the roving is in the spinning frame.

410
00:28:22,380 --> 00:28:24,980
The roving is being fed down into a set of rollers.

411
00:28:24,981 --> 00:28:30,920
If you look at the back roller on the spinning crane, it is turning at a slow rate of speed

412
00:28:30,921 --> 00:28:34,100
compared to the front, which is a high rate of speed.

413
00:28:34,220 --> 00:28:37,620
What that is doing, those rollers are drafting the cotton fibers out.

414
00:28:39,440 --> 00:28:44,620
Then the whirling spindle twists the fibers into yarn, giving them the tensile strength

415
00:28:44,621 --> 00:28:45,800
needed to make fabric.

416
00:28:46,900 --> 00:28:52,440
The mill also uses a newer technology known as open-end spinning, which combines drawing,

417
00:28:52,441 --> 00:28:54,480
roving, and spinning into one process.

418
00:28:54,980 --> 00:28:58,680
But the hottest thing is air jet vortex spinning.

419
00:28:59,280 --> 00:29:01,100
This is air jet spinning.

420
00:29:01,820 --> 00:29:06,040
This is the newest form of spinning in the evolution of spinning process.

421
00:29:06,740 --> 00:29:10,680
We have sliver being fed in the back, drafting rolls.

422
00:29:11,040 --> 00:29:18,240
Instead of a whirling bobbin, putting twists in that strand, here we're inserting twists with a vortex of air.

423
00:29:18,241 --> 00:29:25,600
So we're actually interacting with those individual fibers with compressed air in a mini tornado, if you will.

424
00:29:26,780 --> 00:29:31,380
And should the yarn break, a robot places the broken end back into the air jet.

425
00:29:32,920 --> 00:29:38,240
After the yarns cascade through the spinning machine, they're wound around another set of bobbins,

426
00:29:38,600 --> 00:29:41,200
which are removed automatically and taken to the warp room.

427
00:29:41,201 --> 00:29:49,080
Here, bobbins of the thick, robust yarn used to form the denim warp are wound onto a large

428
00:29:49,081 --> 00:29:50,080
spool.

429
00:29:50,081 --> 00:29:56,980
But before it's woven, the yarn gets its trademark indigo blue.

430
00:29:56,981 --> 00:30:01,920
The indigo dye needs to oxidize in order to turn blue, so the yarn takes several turns

431
00:30:01,921 --> 00:30:06,380
on these skying reels.

432
00:30:06,381 --> 00:30:11,500
Then in a process known as slashing, the beams of yarn pass through a starch bath, which

433
00:30:11,501 --> 00:30:16,140
prepares the yarn for the rigors of high-speed weaving.

434
00:30:16,141 --> 00:30:19,900
Cone Denim boasts one of the largest weave rooms in the world.

435
00:30:19,901 --> 00:30:26,060
The area inside our weave room, you can almost put four football fields inside of it.

436
00:30:26,061 --> 00:30:29,420
It's over a thousand feet long.

437
00:30:29,421 --> 00:30:34,280
Here the great spools of indigo yarn are woven so quickly, it's hard to tell that the centuries-old

438
00:30:34,281 --> 00:30:37,236
practice hasn't changed all that much.

439
00:30:37,237 --> 00:30:41,280
Harnesses still lift the warp yarns up and down, and

440
00:30:41,281 --> 00:30:46,280
the weft yarn, now driven by an air jet, still shoots through the opening and is woven in.

441
00:30:46,281 --> 00:30:50,999
The picks per minute on the air jet is 778 picks per minute.

442
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:53,280
So in a minute's time, you're

443
00:30:53,281 --> 00:30:56,979
going to take the filling across 778 times.

444
00:30:56,980 --> 00:31:01,280
The weft yarn is white, providing the crosshatched

445
00:31:01,281 --> 00:31:06,336
blue-white look that is classic 100% cotton denim.

446
00:31:06,337 --> 00:31:10,280
Blue jeans were introduced in the 1850s

447
00:31:10,281 --> 00:31:11,880
as the working man's garment.

448
00:31:11,881 --> 00:31:15,080
But in the 20th century, they became the signature garment

449
00:31:15,081 --> 00:31:16,455
of America itself.

450
00:31:16,456 --> 00:31:21,880
Today, denim is the largest sector of the cotton market and is still an

451
00:31:21,881 --> 00:31:26,146
American icon from the roundup to the red carpet.

452
00:31:26,147 --> 00:31:29,280
But the uses of cotton go way beyond

453
00:31:29,281 --> 00:31:35,640
clothes. Cotton seed and cellulose feed people, livestock, and industry.

454
00:31:38,640 --> 00:31:43,344
Three quarters of paper money is actually composed of cotton.

455
00:31:43,345 --> 00:31:46,120
Much of this cotton comes from denim

456
00:31:46,121 --> 00:31:49,744
scraps left over from jeans manufacturing.

457
00:31:49,745 --> 00:31:55,440
Cotton will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

458
00:31:59,280 --> 00:32:04,280
Welcome to Planters' Cotton Oil Mill in Pine Bluff, Arkansas.

459
00:32:04,281 --> 00:32:09,280
This is where the fuzzy cotton seed is reduced to its basic elements.

460
00:32:09,281 --> 00:32:14,280
Meal, oil, and tiny bits of fiber called linters.

461
00:32:14,281 --> 00:32:19,280
These basic elements will rise again in literally thousands of different products.

462
00:32:19,281 --> 00:32:22,280
Before there was actually a cotton seed processing plant,

463
00:32:22,281 --> 00:32:25,280
the gins would literally throw the cotton seed away.

464
00:32:25,281 --> 00:32:27,280
They didn't know what to do with it.

465
00:32:27,281 --> 00:32:27,981
do with it.

466
00:32:27,982 --> 00:32:32,160
And so it was more of a byproduct pollution problem than it was anything.

467
00:32:33,300 --> 00:32:36,386
Today's cotton oil mills know what to do with it.

468
00:32:36,387 --> 00:32:39,220
Seeds represent two-thirds the weight of each

469
00:32:39,221 --> 00:32:40,045
cotton bowl.

470
00:32:40,046 --> 00:32:45,680
Every year, the planter's company takes in 600,000 tons of cotton seed from cotton

471
00:32:45,681 --> 00:32:48,022
gins, and none of it will go to waste.

472
00:32:48,023 --> 00:32:51,720
The seeds first work their way through a series of vibrating

473
00:32:51,721 --> 00:32:54,252
sieves to remove dirt or plant trash.

474
00:32:54,253 --> 00:32:57,880
Then they're fed into a machine known as a de-linter,

475
00:32:57,881 --> 00:33:01,180
which uses 200 saw blades to remove the linters from the seeds.

476
00:33:01,181 --> 00:33:07,160
It's almost identical to the cotton gin in that they have saws that remove the lint off

477
00:33:07,161 --> 00:33:07,861
of the seed.

478
00:33:07,862 --> 00:33:11,860
Ours is a little more fine-tuned simply because we're having to really reach

479
00:33:11,861 --> 00:33:17,320
down and grab just a few small fragments of lint off of the seed. On a ton of cotton

480
00:33:17,321 --> 00:33:19,997
seed, we get about 130 pounds of lint.

481
00:33:19,998 --> 00:33:23,800
We make about 90,000 bales of cotton linners per year.

482
00:33:23,801 --> 00:33:29,780
Those bales are about 600 pounds each. The mill makes a first cut, which removes

483
00:33:29,781 --> 00:33:30,960
the longer fibers.

484
00:33:30,961 --> 00:33:36,400
This is our first cut de-linner, and we're taking off 20% of our total lint off our

485
00:33:36,401 --> 00:33:37,101
seeds.

486
00:33:37,102 --> 00:33:42,360
They use this lint in stuffing some couches, STP oil filters, and fine writing paper.

487
00:33:42,361 --> 00:33:47,860
A second cut removes the shorter fibers still stubbornly clinging to the seed.

488
00:33:47,861 --> 00:33:53,560
The second cut goes into a lot of film, more of the refined stuff, electronics and things

489
00:33:53,561 --> 00:33:56,080
like that because it's a lot smaller fiber.

490
00:33:56,081 --> 00:33:59,360
The smaller fiber is easier to work with.

491
00:33:59,361 --> 00:34:03,540
Cotton linters are valued in such surprising applications because they are nature's purest

492
00:34:03,541 --> 00:34:08,760
source of cellulose, the natural polymer that makes up plant cell walls.

493
00:34:08,761 --> 00:34:14,160
In film-forming applications, cellulose is dissolved in an organic solvent.

494
00:34:15,440 --> 00:34:19,660
The cellulose polymer bonds with the solvent to form a strong plastic film.

495
00:34:21,720 --> 00:34:26,420
Cotton cellulose has long been used to make cinematic film and celluloid history.

496
00:34:28,060 --> 00:34:33,200
In the early 20th century, scientists discovered how to produce nitrocellulose,

497
00:34:33,640 --> 00:34:37,180
which proved useful as both a highly explosive weapon of war

498
00:34:37,181 --> 00:34:40,520
and later as a protective layer and safety glass.

499
00:34:42,080 --> 00:34:46,220
Back at the mill, it's time to squeeze every last drop of value from the cotton seeds.

500
00:34:48,060 --> 00:34:51,220
Once stripped of the linters, the seeds go through decorticators,

501
00:34:51,700 --> 00:34:54,700
where mechanized knives shear the seeds' meat from the holes.

502
00:34:55,300 --> 00:34:57,620
We take those meats from the separation room,

503
00:34:57,860 --> 00:35:01,920
and we prep them by adding a little water, running them through some large cookers,

504
00:35:02,400 --> 00:35:06,980
and after we cook them, they come out in a pellet-type form over to our extraction.

505
00:35:07,180 --> 00:35:10,780
where they're washed with a solvent.

506
00:35:10,880 --> 00:35:13,480
After the solvent extracts the oil from the meal,

507
00:35:13,580 --> 00:35:17,320
the mixture is heated and the solvent is flashed off.

508
00:35:17,420 --> 00:35:20,120
What's left is cottonseed oil.

509
00:35:20,220 --> 00:35:22,820
The biggest majority of cottonseed oil

510
00:35:22,920 --> 00:35:26,640
is used in the potato chip plants throughout the nation

511
00:35:26,740 --> 00:35:28,840
to fry their chips in.

512
00:35:28,940 --> 00:35:31,740
It's a very stable, long-life oil

513
00:35:31,840 --> 00:35:34,880
because it does not require hydrogenation,

514
00:35:34,980 --> 00:35:36,440
and it's zero in trans fat,

515
00:35:36,441 --> 00:35:38,940
which is the newest thing on the health radar these days.

516
00:35:38,941 --> 00:35:43,940
So, you know, cottonseed oil is very popular in the snack food industry.

517
00:35:43,941 --> 00:35:48,940
The leftover cottonseed meal is very popular with cattle.

518
00:35:48,941 --> 00:35:52,940
Cotton seed is very well known for increasing the butterfat in milk,

519
00:35:52,941 --> 00:35:54,940
and dairy operators throughout the nation,

520
00:35:54,941 --> 00:35:57,940
they receive a premium for that butterfat contents in there.

521
00:35:57,941 --> 00:36:00,440
So usually some of your larger dairies,

522
00:36:00,441 --> 00:36:05,440
they're feeding about 6 pounds per head per day of cottonseed.

523
00:36:06,440 --> 00:36:13,060
Ironically, not long ago, the chemical industry which cotton cellulose helped to create threatened

524
00:36:13,061 --> 00:36:16,680
the Wonder Fibers' dominance in its number one industry, clothing.

525
00:36:16,681 --> 00:36:21,500
In the 1960s and through about the middle part of the 1970s, cotton was losing a lot

526
00:36:21,501 --> 00:36:26,200
of market share to the synthetic fibers that came out with some polyesters and other fibers.

527
00:36:26,201 --> 00:36:32,620
We were down to maybe 35% of the apparel market from close to 70% in the early 60s.

528
00:36:32,621 --> 00:36:35,840
But cotton has gradually regained his throne.

529
00:36:35,841 --> 00:36:40,800
And while it was denim that led the charge in cotton's restoration, the epitome of comfort,

530
00:36:40,801 --> 00:36:46,200
the 100% cotton t-shirt, has done more than its share.

531
00:36:46,201 --> 00:36:51,380
In Los Angeles, American Apparel has become one of the nation's fastest growing companies

532
00:36:51,381 --> 00:36:52,620
on the back of the t-shirt.

533
00:36:52,621 --> 00:36:54,820
We're a completely vertical company.

534
00:36:54,821 --> 00:36:59,280
The only thing that we require is a good quality spun cotton yarn.

535
00:36:59,281 --> 00:37:04,860
We can take that yarn then, we can knit it here, and we can bleach, dye, finish the fabric here.

536
00:37:04,960 --> 00:37:10,960
We cut our fabric into pieces, we sew our pieces into garments, and we distribute throughout the world,

537
00:37:11,140 --> 00:37:14,000
all out of this facility in downtown Los Angeles.

538
00:37:14,001 --> 00:37:17,020
And in 2005, that's pretty unique.

539
00:37:18,080 --> 00:37:21,700
Unlike denim, which is woven, all the garments here are knit.

540
00:37:21,701 --> 00:37:28,940
A modern-day machine knits up to 120 ends of cotton yarn into a web of interlocking loops

541
00:37:28,941 --> 00:37:32,100
that form the fabric.

542
00:37:32,101 --> 00:37:36,260
Fabric assumes a tubular shape as it's wound into rolls.

543
00:37:36,261 --> 00:37:38,840
Fans keep stray cotton fibers out of the way.

544
00:37:38,841 --> 00:37:43,820
While not as strong as the grid-like structure of a weave, the interlacing loops of a knit

545
00:37:43,821 --> 00:37:45,260
are inherently more flexible.

546
00:37:45,261 --> 00:37:47,940
Woven goods are much more rigid.

547
00:37:47,941 --> 00:37:52,380
Goods are built to perform and they're built to move and they're built to stretch.

548
00:37:52,381 --> 00:37:55,340
That's why most t-shirts are knit.

549
00:37:55,341 --> 00:38:00,880
We're putting four plies of tubular fabric into the front end of the machine.

550
00:38:00,881 --> 00:38:01,880
Machine then takes over.

551
00:38:01,881 --> 00:38:04,980
It's going to pull those four plies into the machine.

552
00:38:04,981 --> 00:38:06,380
It's going to align them.

553
00:38:06,381 --> 00:38:10,060
It's going to make sure everything is balanced within.

554
00:38:10,061 --> 00:38:14,320
It'll stamp out the pieces.

555
00:38:14,321 --> 00:38:20,520
So literally here, every 9.2 seconds, we'll get four t-shirts.

556
00:38:20,521 --> 00:38:24,780
And of course the sleeves to go with it.

557
00:38:24,781 --> 00:38:31,500
Highly skilled sewing teams stitch on the collars, sleeves, and hem bottoms in 90 seconds

558
00:38:31,501 --> 00:38:33,560
flat.

559
00:38:33,561 --> 00:38:38,960
Like blue jeans, t-shirts have secured their place in American and global culture by delivering

560
00:38:38,961 --> 00:38:44,120
equal doses of comfort and style.

561
00:38:44,121 --> 00:38:49,700
Thanks to the success of such timeless garments, the future of cotton is unlimited, especially

562
00:38:49,701 --> 00:38:52,200
with a little help from nanotechnology.

563
00:38:54,920 --> 00:39:00,280
Cotton linters are used as a binding material in a wide range of products, including paint,

564
00:39:00,820 --> 00:39:05,560
lipstick, nail polish, toothpaste, ice cream, and hot dog casings.

565
00:39:06,640 --> 00:39:10,840
Cotton will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

566
00:39:14,120 --> 00:39:21,120
Cotton is prized for its ability to absorb moisture, making it comfortable and cool.

567
00:39:21,121 --> 00:39:28,120
That's also made it less than ideal when the weather turns rough, until now.

568
00:39:28,121 --> 00:39:33,120
This is storm denim. This is our latest development in textile chemistry.

569
00:39:33,121 --> 00:39:38,120
It's just like regular denim, except it provides this whole layer of protection.

570
00:39:38,121 --> 00:39:43,120
And you can actually see what happens here as we spray that garment.

571
00:39:43,121 --> 00:39:45,800
you can see the repellency that you would get in a storm.

572
00:39:46,200 --> 00:39:47,600
The denim has been waterproofed

573
00:39:47,601 --> 00:39:50,060
with a layer of microscopic protective particles.

574
00:39:50,480 --> 00:39:53,640
We've got particles here that are about 10 nanometers

575
00:39:53,641 --> 00:39:55,780
to 100 nanometers that are applied,

576
00:39:56,100 --> 00:39:58,760
and they actually will sit on the surface of the fiber

577
00:39:58,761 --> 00:40:01,180
and be bonded to the surface of the fiber.

578
00:40:01,181 --> 00:40:02,720
But we can actually do this

579
00:40:02,721 --> 00:40:05,260
without altering the breathability of the fabric.

580
00:40:05,700 --> 00:40:07,700
We still maintain good vapor transport,

581
00:40:07,980 --> 00:40:09,760
and we still maintain the comfort features

582
00:40:09,761 --> 00:40:12,480
and all of the normal things that people come to know

583
00:40:12,481 --> 00:40:14,180
and love in a denim fabric.

584
00:40:14,820 --> 00:40:17,860
But Storm Denim is only the latest successful use

585
00:40:17,861 --> 00:40:19,380
of nanochemistry on cotton.

586
00:40:20,900 --> 00:40:23,820
Just as cotton textiles started the Industrial Revolution,

587
00:40:25,600 --> 00:40:28,820
stain-resistant and wrinkle-free cotton garments

588
00:40:28,821 --> 00:40:31,120
were among the first products to test the waters

589
00:40:31,121 --> 00:40:32,520
of the nanotech revolution.

590
00:40:33,540 --> 00:40:34,920
We can do all those things to cotton,

591
00:40:34,921 --> 00:40:37,260
so it stays young.

592
00:40:37,400 --> 00:40:40,360
It's a 6,000-year-old fiber, but it stays young and viable.

593
00:40:40,361 --> 00:40:45,360
Cotton and technology will continue to get along famously.

594
00:40:45,361 --> 00:40:52,360
More problematic is cotton's relationship to the global economy and environment.

595
00:40:52,361 --> 00:40:59,360
Although cotton makes up only a small percentage of the world's crops, it uses 25% of the world's insecticides.

596
00:40:59,361 --> 00:41:07,360
Cotton, genetically engineered to resist pests, has greatly reduced the amount of dangerous chemicals sprayed over fields.

597
00:41:07,361 --> 00:41:10,634
fields, and organic cotton has made a small dent.

598
00:41:10,635 --> 00:41:13,040
But the environmental damage wreaked

599
00:41:13,041 --> 00:41:16,616
by cotton cultivation is a long way from resolved.

600
00:41:16,617 --> 00:41:19,620
Meanwhile, the cotton economy finds itself

601
00:41:19,621 --> 00:41:24,710
once again in a state of rapid change, especially in America. The U.S.

602
00:41:24,711 --> 00:41:26,600
used to control the cotton

603
00:41:26,601 --> 00:41:28,454
trade from dirt to shirt.

604
00:41:28,455 --> 00:41:33,720
Now nearly all textile mills and garment factories have moved overseas,

605
00:41:33,721 --> 00:41:38,400
From China to the Caribbean, along with tens of thousands of jobs.

606
00:41:38,401 --> 00:41:42,840
But a few companies are bucking the trend and thriving.

607
00:41:42,841 --> 00:41:48,560
American Apparel uses a vertical integration model that would make Henry Ford proud.

608
00:41:48,561 --> 00:41:52,880
The Los Angeles factory employs 4,000 workers.

609
00:41:52,881 --> 00:42:02,540
Downstairs from the cutting and sewing departments are marketing, graphics, and a photo shoot

610
00:42:02,541 --> 00:42:07,351
in progress. Even the models are plucked from different departments.

611
00:42:07,352 --> 00:42:09,120
By aggressively targeting

612
00:42:09,121 --> 00:42:14,320
a young, hip market, American Apparel has seen revenues increase 12-fold in the last

613
00:42:14,321 --> 00:42:18,040
five years, all while offering good wages and benefits.

614
00:42:18,041 --> 00:42:22,746
We have the highest-earning apparel workers in the world.

615
00:42:22,747 --> 00:42:25,140
People ask, how can we afford

616
00:42:25,141 --> 00:42:27,743
to do this? I ask, how can we afford not to?

617
00:42:27,744 --> 00:42:30,700
What is the actual cost of manufacturing offshore,

618
00:42:30,701 --> 00:42:32,700
What do you lose when you don't have product?

619
00:42:32,840 --> 00:42:34,740
What do you lose when you don't have quality?

620
00:42:34,900 --> 00:42:37,340
What do you lose when you lose customer confidence?

621
00:42:38,720 --> 00:42:42,680
Back in North Carolina, where textile mills have become an endangered species,

622
00:42:43,100 --> 00:42:45,500
the white oak plant continues to thrive.

623
00:42:46,440 --> 00:42:49,680
One of the plant's strategies combines tradition with technology

624
00:42:49,681 --> 00:42:52,480
to recreate the blue jeans of a bygone era.

625
00:42:52,860 --> 00:42:56,340
Back then you had yarn that was made with a lot of irregularities.

626
00:42:56,341 --> 00:43:03,980
And then worn over a period of time, the indigo would start to abrade off, and you'd see all this yarn character.

627
00:43:04,780 --> 00:43:11,860
Well, with the advent of the modern equipment, we made much smoother, more regular yarns, lost that vintage character.

628
00:43:12,400 --> 00:43:14,740
Well, today, we're able to recreate it.

629
00:43:15,080 --> 00:43:21,660
We have units on our spinning frames that allow us to go back and recreate those vintage-type yarns

630
00:43:21,661 --> 00:43:26,520
with this type of character in them that, when woven in the fabric, give the vintage appearance.

631
00:43:27,700 --> 00:43:32,000
The design team uses vintage looms and the experience of its employees

632
00:43:32,001 --> 00:43:34,380
to recreate the much-coveted vintage look.

633
00:43:35,260 --> 00:43:37,580
White Oak has approximately 1,000 employees.

634
00:43:38,000 --> 00:43:41,840
Over 40% of them have more than 25 years of seniority.

635
00:43:42,500 --> 00:43:47,080
If you look at the total years of experience that this plant has,

636
00:43:47,081 --> 00:43:53,080
you're looking at it in excess of 15,000 years of experience.

637
00:43:54,260 --> 00:43:56,840
You can't just find that anywhere.

638
00:43:58,280 --> 00:44:02,820
In the 21st century, no matter where in the world cotton has grown,

639
00:44:03,780 --> 00:44:06,760
ginned, woven, and sewn,

640
00:44:07,400 --> 00:44:09,660
it will continue to clothe the world.

641
00:44:10,920 --> 00:44:14,920
Cotton has woven itself into wars and revolutions

642
00:44:14,921 --> 00:44:17,720
and the very fabric of history.

643
00:44:17,820 --> 00:44:20,420
It really does make a good yarn.

